b/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186176734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176151 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176151 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Generalized Eigenvectors of Specific Ranks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176151 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176151 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176151 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "76d0033d-cf7a-5da1-968b-686218d7353c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176671 #edb/ref 1061 24944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52289fce-81d4-5acc-b253-84ae58296946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176732 #edb/ref 1061 33802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "821a514d-0d12-514d-b767-9be69ad9d2a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176733 #edb/ref 1061 33803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c51ce26a-138c-5a62-b9bc-d912ad4d6186" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176734 #edb/ref 1061 33804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0121c7c5-cde8-580a-a3e3-842d3e2d6bbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies\n\n$$(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (A - \\lambda I)^{k-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate generalized eigenvectors of a specific rank for a given matrix $A.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nSuppose we're given that $\\lambda = -3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ and that this eigenvalue has corresponding generalized eigenvectors of rank $2.$ How do we find one of these generalized eigenvectors?\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -3,$ we need to find a vector $\\mathbf v$ that satisfies\n\n$$(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad (A +3I)^{} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ First, we have $$A + 3I = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$$ Computing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent: $$(A+3I)^2= [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ This means that *any* vector in $\\mathbb R^2$ is a solution to $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as $$\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A +3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We have $$A+3I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$$ Converting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get $$A+3I\\sim[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$$ This means the solution to $(A +3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_2 = 0$, where $v_1$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A + 3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as $$\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector\n\n$${\\color{red}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$$\n\nsatisfies $(A +3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A +3 I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nHence, ${\\color{red}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1065 5423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3b0674e-a304-5e37-b45a-b512efd3ba3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176996 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Generalized Eigenvectors of Specific Ranks" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186411196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411158 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "12310c88-a119-5240-a679-c24d59975557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411159 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411159 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411159 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0503c459-8cf4-58f4-b1e7-4c9d8ddb7982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411160 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "143e9898-e76d-592c-b81a-be3942903090" #e